description:
OutboxStore::finish_message_delivery() (kith-store/src/outbox.rs:287) first runs a SELECT to check whether the message row exists and is not already delivered, then conditionally runs an UPDATE. This SELECT + branch + UPDATE pattern is more verbose than necessary.

The reason given in comments (line 308-316) is ordering: the state counter must be advanced before the UPDATE that stamps state_version on the row. The SELECT probe is used to decide whether to call advance_state_counter_in_tx at all.

A cleaner alternative: run the UPDATE first (it already has AND delivery_state != 'delivered' in its WHERE clause), check rows_affected, and call advance_state_counter_in_tx only when rows_affected > 0. This eliminates the SELECT entirely:

  let rows = tx.execute("UPDATE messages SET delivery_state = ?1 WHERE id = ?2 AND delivery_state != 'delivered'", ...)?;
  if rows > 0 {
    let v = advance_state_counter_in_tx(&tx, "message")?;
    tx.execute("UPDATE messages SET state_version = ?1 WHERE id = ?2", params![v, entry.message_id])?;
  }

This requires two UPDATEs in the delivery path instead of SELECT + UPDATE, but eliminates the existence probe and is easier to follow.
